ndment that the Opposition moved by narrowing the timing scope by two years, omitting reference to the MACH Energy v Denman court cases and giving the Minister discretion by inserting the words \"primary coal policy development documents\" rather than leaving it open-ended. The Minister made the valid point that for the two mines that would remain—the Mount Pleasant operation and Hunter Valley Operations—we are talking about a total of 20,000 documents, which would burst open the Mookhey library and be completely unworkable in terms of administration. Even though the Opposition has made an attempt to target the SO 52 and limit its open-ended nature, with the consent of the mover, according to the Government—and I can only take the Minister at her word, on her departmental advice—we are still talking about 20,000 documents, which is a fishing trip on steroids. The Government will need a bigger boat, quite frankly, to cope with that scope.","result":"negative","slug":"division-nsw-lc-2026-10-21-1","source_url":"https://api.parliament.nsw.gov.au/api/hansard/search/daily/fragment/HANSARD-1820781676-106125"}
